# A card without MSIs: QEMU's Intel 82540EM ("e1000") interrupts through
# its INTx, a level-triggered line of the I/O APIC. The device manager finds
# where the firmware wires it (its _PRT), the driver VM gets the line, and
# the guest's ACPI tables route the card's INTx to it: Linux's e1000 drives
# the card through the line, which stays masked from when it fires until
# Linux's end-of-interrupt. The card attaches to the network service and
# gets an address by DHCP; nettest checks ping, TCP and HTTP through it,
# against a server of the host's (no Internet needed).
net ethernet
nic e1000
nettest local
fail-on "PANIC"
fail-on "driver-virtual-machine: processor"
fail-on "nettest: FAIL"
wait-serial "goes to the driver VM" 60
wait-serial "INTA# on GSI" 30
wait-serial "net: eth0 (e1000, pci 00:" 120
wait-serial "net: eth0: attached as eth0" 60
wait-serial "nettest: PASS" 300
reject-serial "cannot be routed"
reject-serial "panicked"
